altairsim.log

# The assembled distribution. tools/build-package.sh writes dist/altairsim-<ver>-<target>/
# and the archive beside it -- an OUTPUT of the tree, never an input to it. The directory
# itself is TRACKED (via dist/README.md) so it always exists after a checkout: dist/ is
# also the release COLLECTION point (DISTRIBUTION.md 6), and a worker must be able to scp into
# it before the coordinator has built anything. Contents stay ignored; the README does not.
/dist/*
!/dist/README.md

# Binaries CI built, pulled down by tools/fetch-ci-binaries.sh. Scratch: overwritten on
# every fetch, read by nothing in the build or the tests.
/artifacts/

# The built documents. THE MARKDOWN IS THE SOURCE; the PDF is an artifact of it, of the
# binary (the reference chapters are printed from the program) and of a browser.
#
# The USER-FACING PDFs ARE TRACKED. tools/build-docs.sh makes docs/altairsim-manual.pdf and
# docs/altairsim-devguide.pdf, and they are versioned deliverables: they ship in the
# distribution zip beside the binary, and a fresh clone should carry the manual a reader can
# open without a pandoc-and-a-browser toolchain to build it. So they are committed on purpose
# (regenerate and re-commit them when the docs change), and NOT listed here.
#
# The line that is NOT ours to track is the SCANNED REFERENCE MANUALS in reference/ (below):
# vendor datasheets, tens of MB each, and burned into this repo's history twice already. That
# is the "no PDFs in the reference directory" rule -- our documentation is fine, their scans
# are not. The intermediate HTML the browser prints from stays an artifact too.
docs/*.html
.altairsim_history

# Disk/tape images are large and usually not ours to redistribute.
# Commit them deliberately with `git add -f` if a test genuinely needs one.
*.dsk
*.DSK
*.cas
*.img

# disks/ IS AN ALLOWLIST, and it is deliberately the other way round from everything
# above (Patrick, 2026-07-13: "the only files we need tracked in the /disks directory
# are the TOML files").
#
# What lives there is somebody else's: 20 MB of CP/M images, vendor ReadMes, and the
# .PRN listings that come with them. A denylist would have to guess at every extension
# a future download brings, and a single `git add -A` on a miss puts 20 MB in history
# forever -- which has already happened once in this repo, with reference/, and had to
# be rewritten out. So NOTHING under disks/ is tracked unless it is named here.
#
# Four kinds of thing earn a line:
#   - the machine files, which ARE ours;
#   - one README per directory, saying where to download the images (deramp.com) and
#     how to run them -- so a fresh clone knows exactly what it is missing and why;
#   - the .ASM listings the BOARDS WERE BUILT FROM. Those are first-hand period sources
#     under DESIGN.md 0.1 and are cited by path in docs/boards/*.md. Losing them would
#     mean the 88-DCDD's register map, its timing, and the Tarbell's grounded-E30
#     finding all cite a file that is not there.
#   - THREE BOOTABLE CP/M IMAGES, named one at a time below (an 8" floppy, and the two
#     minidisks that only boot as a pair). They are the exception to the paragraph above
#     and they are here on purpose (2026-07-18): a test whose input has to be downloaded
#     first is a test that skips, and four acceptance tests had been skipping on every
#     fresh clone and in all of CI. 480 KB buys them back, and the 8" one carries DDT.COM
#     and our own R/W/HDIR.COM. See docs/sources.md for provenance, and
#     tools/fetch-disk-images.sh for the images that are still fetched.
disks/**
!disks/**/
!disks/**/*.toml
!disks/**/README.md
!disks/mits-88mds/cpm22/CPM56K-1.DSK
!disks/mits-88mds/cpm22/CPM56K-2.DSK
!disks/mits-88dcdd/cpm22/buffered/BIOS.ASM
!disks/mits-88dcdd/cpm22/buffered/BOOT.ASM
!disks/mits-88dcdd/cpm22/8mb/BIOS.ASM
!disks/mits-88dcdd/cpm22/8mb/BOOT.ASM
!disks/mits-88dcdd/cpm22/8mb/FORMAT8M.ASM
!disks/mits-88mds/cpm22/BIOS.ASM
!disks/mits-88mds/cpm22/BOOT.ASM
!disks/tarbell-sd/cpm22/buffered/BIOS (1).ASM

# tests/media/ is the simulator's own test media -- every image in it is there because a test
# reads it, so it is committed on purpose. examples/ is never a simulator fixture -- an example
# is tested only to prove the example works -- so a test that needs a disk takes its own copy here.
!tests/media/**/*.dsk
!tests/media/**/*.DSK
!tests/media/**/*.img

# examples/ IS THE SHIPPED SET, and it is an allowlist for the same reason disks/ is.
#
# These are the examples that ship -- cpm, basic4k, basic1, debugger and ai-mcp -- and they
# are what goes in the package. They are tested only as examples (acceptance-examples); the
# simulator's own tests take their media from tests/media/ (above). The rules above would hide
# most of it: `*.dsk` swallows the CP/M images. More examples are at
# https://altairsim.com (2026-09-30).
#
# So each shipped file is named. If you add one and git does not see it, that is this
# block doing its job -- add the line.
examples/**
!examples/**/
!examples/**/*.toml
!examples/**/*.ini
!examples/**/README.md
# Every example's README is rendered to a sibling README.pdf (tools/build-docs.sh, committed
# by CI) so a file manager with no Markdown viewer can read it, and so it travels in the
# package. One glob covers all of them, at any depth.
!examples/**/README.pdf
!examples/cpm/cpm22b23-56k.dsk
!examples/basic4k/4K BASIC Ver 3-1.tap
!examples/basic4k/4K BASIC Ver 3-1.wav
!examples/basic4k/4K BASIC (Kansas City).wav
!examples/basic4k/LDR4K31.HEX
!examples/basic1/BASIC Ver 1-0.tap
!examples/basic1/BASIC Ver 1-0.wav
!examples/basic1/LOAD10.HEX
!examples/debugger/HELLO.ASM
!examples/debugger/HELLO.PRN
!examples/debugger/HELLO.HEX
# examples/ai-mcp ships the CP/M disk (with HELLO.ASM baked on for an assistant to build over
# MCP); HELLO.ASM is here too as the pristine planted bug -- kept to rebuild the disk, stripped
# from the package like every other .ASM beside an image (tools/build-package.sh).
!examples/ai-mcp/cpm22b23-56k.dsk
!examples/ai-mcp/HELLO.ASM

# Scanned manuals and datasheets. Same reasoning as the disk images, and then
# some: they are enormous (the 88-DCDD manual alone is 52 MB) and they are not
# ours. They are the SOURCE the boards were built from, not part of the build --
# keep them here on disk, cite them by filename in the code, and let
# docs/sources.md say where each one came from. See [altair-hardware-sources].
#
# This was decided once, and then a `git add -A` put 83 MB of them in history
# anyway; that history has been rewritten out. If you find yourself typing
# `git add -f reference/...`, you are about to do it a third time.
#
# The ONE exception, allowlist-style like disks/ above: the *.md files here are
# OURS -- distilled, text-only emulation references written FROM the scans, each
# citing its source PDF by filename. "Our documentation is fine, their scans are
# not." Those are tracked; the scans themselves stay ignored.
reference/*
!reference/*.md

# CTest scratch output
Testing/Temporary/

# The `lineprinter` machine's capture file. `machines/lineprinter.toml` connects the
# 88-C700 to `file:printout.txt`, so merely running that machine from the repo root
# drops one here -- and an empty one got committed once by a `git add -A` (37afae6).
printout.txt

# The `parallel` machine's capture file, for the same reason: `machines/parallel.toml`
# connects the 88-PIO's `out` line to `file:parallel-out.txt`.
parallel-out.txt

# .claude/ IS MOSTLY LOCAL, AND ONE DIRECTORY IN IT IS NOT.
#
# settings.local.json is this machine's permission grants, scheduled_tasks.lock is a runtime
# file, and worktrees/ is agent scratch -- none of it is repository content, and a `git add -A`
# would sweep every one of them into a commit (the same mistake printout.txt records above).
# So the default is EXCLUDE, and the exception is named.
#
# .claude/skills/ IS TRACKED ON PURPOSE. It holds the altairsim skill -- the briefing an
# assistant reads to drive a guest over MCP -- and Claude Code discovers a project skill at
# exactly that path, so committing it is what makes a fresh clone work with no setup. It is
# also the packaged skill's source (docs/package.map ships it as `skills/`).
#
# The pattern must exclude the CONTENTS (`.claude/*`), not the directory (`.claude/`): git
# does not descend into an excluded directory, so a `!` re-include underneath one never fires.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/

# This site's release machines: addresses, ssh users, serial port names. The tracked
# distribution.conf.example is the template; each site keeps its own copy under this name.
distribution.conf
